# Bobby frontend — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (git-ignored, highest precedence) and adjust:
#   cp frontend/.env.example frontend/.env.local
#
# `.env*` is git-ignored; this template is explicitly re-allowed so it stays
# committable. Never put a server-side secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* var — those
# are inlined into the client bundle and are public by definition.
#
# On Vercel, set these in Project Settings → Environment Variables for
# Production, Preview AND Development. NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are read at BUILD
# time and baked into the static bundle, so adding a var requires a redeploy —
# editing it in the dashboard alone will not change a running deployment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED — backend base URLs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The browser talks to Render DIRECTLY. There is no Next.js proxy: the frontend
# and backend are separate origins, and a serverless runtime cannot proxy a
# WebSocket upgrade anyway.
#
# Provide the ORIGIN only. The app appends the endpoint paths itself:
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL  ->  ${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL}/api/start-workflow
#     NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL   ->  ${NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL}/ws/agent-stream
#
# Trailing slashes are stripped, so "https://x.onrender.com/" is fine.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL may be given as https:// — it is upgraded to wss:// so the
# secure page does not attempt an insecure mixed-content socket. Set it to the
# Render https origin; you do not need to hand-write the wss:// scheme.

# Local development (commented out so an unset value fails closed):
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Production — the Render service origin:
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://bobby-backend.onrender.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_WS_URL=https://bobby-backend.onrender.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both are mandatory — the app fails closed without them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If either var is empty or unparseable, agentStream.ts substitutes a sentinel
# that can never resolve. Submitting the form then surfaces a configuration
# error instead of firing a same-origin request that would 404 against the
# static Vercel deployment, and the WebSocket is never opened. This is
# deliberate: a silent fallback is what produces confusing 404s in production.
#
# next.config.ts also derives the CSP `connect-src` allowlist from these two
# origins, so the Content-Security-Policy admits exactly the hosts the app
# contacts and nothing else (no wildcard, no bare ws:/https:).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No other variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKEND_ORIGIN and the NEXT_PUBLIC_WORKFLOW_START_URL /
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_STREAM_URL overrides have been REMOVED along with the
# same-origin rewrites. A reverse-proxy deployment is no longer supported; the
# backend must be reachable at a real origin that sets CORS headers and
# validates the WebSocket Origin against FRONTEND_ORIGIN.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# - Serve over HTTPS. The CSP sent by next.config.ts includes
#   Strict-Transport-Security in production, honoured only on secure origins.
# - The Render backend must have FRONTEND_ORIGIN set to this app's public
#   origin (e.g. https://bobby.vercel.app) or the browser will be rejected:
#   CORS will omit the header and the WebSocket will close with 1008.
# - Render free instances sleep after inactivity and drop open agent streams;
#   use a paid instance for reliable streaming.
